<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thietmar of Merseburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">975–<a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thietmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> (975–1018) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Merseburg in 1009. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Chronicon, covering the Ottonian kings and emperors up to 1018, is a principal source for eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>. It includes a well-known description of the Lutician federation and its pagan temple at Riedegost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg.jpg" alt="Thietmar of Merseburg" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald Rossa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tafel_1009_Bischof_Thietmar_v._Merseburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thietmar was born on 25 July 975, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Siegfried I the Older of Walbeck and his wife Kunigunde, daughter of Henry I the Bald, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>. Two of his great-grandfathers, both named Liuthar, were killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> at the Battle of Lenzen. His father fought alongside Margrave Odo against Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I of Poland</a> at the Battle of Cedynia in 972. His uncle Lothair of Walbeck was margrave of the Northern March from 983 until his death in 1003.</p><p>Baptized in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, Thietmar prepared for a church career. He was educated at the chapter of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Servatius</a> at Quedlinburg Abbey, from 987 at the Benedictine abbey of Berge near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and from 1 November 990 at the Magdeburg cathedral school, together with his relative Bruno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a>. He knew the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, Lucan and Macrobius. In 994 he was for a time a hostage in the hands of the Norsemen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of his parents Thietmar inherited large parts of the Walbeck estates, and in 1002 he became provost of the family monastery founded by his grandfather Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. Archbishop Tagino of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> ordained him priest on 21 December 1004. In 1009, through Tagino's intercession, he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, a diocese that King Henry II had re-established in 1004. Thietmar worked for its full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a>. A loyal supporter of the German kingship, he rarely interfered in political affairs. He died on 1 December 1018 and was buried in Merseburg Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Between 1012 and 1018 Thietmar composed the Chronicon Thietmari in eight books, covering the years 908 to 1018 and the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, the three Ottos and Henry II. The first three books rest largely on earlier chronicles, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>'s Res gestae Saxonicae and the Annales Quedlinburgenses. The fourth, on Otto III, contains much original matter, and the last four are his own narrative and the main source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> history under Henry II. They also give information found nowhere else about the Slavic tribes east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. It includes a detailed history of the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> and of the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> and Poles.</p><p>The surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> shows amendments and insertions made by Thietmar after its completion. It was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in 1570 and badly damaged when the city was bombed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>; only a few folios remain intact, but a complete facsimile had been published by L. Schmidt in 1905. His statement that the Gero Cross in Cologne Cathedral was commissioned by Archbishop Gero, who died in 976, was long dismissed by art historians and was confirmed by dendrochronology in 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thietmar of Merseburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For the West Slavs between the Vistula and the Elbe, the chronicles of <b>Thietmar of Merseburg</b>, Adam of Bremen and Helmold, the lives of Otto of Bamberg, Saxo Grammaticus’ Gesta Danorum and the Knýtlinga saga describe temples and cults in detail.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The federation had no prince; decisions were taken by assemblies of the tribes, and <b>Thietmar of Merseburg</b> describes the temple of Radegast (Svarożic) at Rethra, whose priests consulted oracles.</blockquote><a href="/state/lutician-federation">Lutician Federation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bishop <b>Thietmar of Merseburg</b>, from 1009 to 1018, wrote a chronicle that is a key source on the Ottonian empire, the Slavs and early Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-merseburg">Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.15,9.95&amp;year=1002" aria-label="Hildesheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thietmar of Merseburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 5630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 5630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.15,9.95&amp;year=1002">What was here in 1002? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
